-- Migration: Question generation — AUD batch 10 (30 questions)
-- Date: 2026-02-23
-- Topic: Misstatements and Control Deficiencies
-- Difficulty: 9E / 15M / 6H
-- Bloom's: L1:8 / L2:9 / L3:8 / L4:5

BEGIN;

INSERT INTO questions (section_id, topic, stem, choices, correct_index, explanation, difficulty, cognitive_level)
VALUES

-- Q1: medium / L2 — Clearly trivial threshold application
(1, 'Misstatements and Control Deficiencies', 'During the audit of Aurora Technology Solutions, the engagement team establishes overall materiality at $200,000 and performance materiality at $150,000. Under AU-C 450, the team must also determine a threshold below which misstatements are considered clearly trivial. Which application of the clearly trivial threshold is most appropriate?', '["Set the threshold at $100,000, which is 50% of overall materiality", "Set the threshold equal to performance materiality at $150,000", "Set the threshold at $10,000, an amount below which misstatements need not be accumulated for evaluation", "Set the threshold at zero to ensure all misstatements are accumulated"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Under AU-C 450.A2, clearly trivial misstatements are those the auditor designates as amounts below which misstatements need not be accumulated because the auditor expects the total would not have a material effect on the financial statements. A threshold of $10,000 (5% of materiality) appropriately reflects an amount well below overall materiality. While choice A sets the threshold at 50% of materiality, this would be too high and could allow significant misstatements to go undetected.', 'medium', 2),

-- Q2: hard / L3 — Rollover vs iron curtain approaches for prior-year misstatements
(1, 'Misstatements and Control Deficiencies', 'Granite Peak Industries has a $30,000 uncorrected overstatement of depreciation expense carried forward from 2024. In 2025, overall materiality is $250,000. The auditor is evaluating whether this prior-year misstatement affects the current-year financial statements. Analyze how the rollover and iron curtain approaches would differ in evaluating this misstatement.', '["Both approaches would evaluate only the $30,000 income statement effect, reaching the same conclusion on materiality", "The rollover approach evaluates the $30,000 current-year income statement effect, while the iron curtain approach evaluates the cumulative $60,000 balance sheet effect", "The iron curtain approach ignores prior-year misstatements entirely, while the rollover approach accumulates them", "The rollover approach evaluates cumulative balance sheet effects, while the iron curtain approach evaluates only the current-year originating amount"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Under AU-C 450.A16, the rollover approach quantifies a misstatement as the amount originating in the current period, while the iron curtain approach quantifies based on the cumulative effect on the current-period balance sheet. For a repeating depreciation error, the rollover approach considers only the $30,000 current-year effect on income, whereas the iron curtain approach considers the total $60,000 cumulative effect on accumulated depreciation. Choice D is incorrect because it reverses the definitions of the two approaches.', 'hard', 3),

-- Q3: hard / L4 — Management override and opinion evaluation
(1, 'Misstatements and Control Deficiencies', 'During the audit of Meridian Financial Group, the senior auditor discovers that the CFO manually overrode an automated revenue cutoff control in December 2025, recording $175,000 in revenue before the performance obligation was satisfied. Overall materiality is $400,000. The CFO claims the override was a one-time correction for a system error, but no documentation supports this assertion. Evaluate the auditor''s best course of action.', '["Accept the CFO''s explanation since the $175,000 misstatement is below overall materiality and does not require further action", "Propose an adjusting entry for $175,000 but issue an unmodified opinion regardless of management''s response since the amount is below materiality", "Report the override to those charged with governance as a significant deficiency but do not consider it when evaluating the opinion", "Evaluate the override as a potential indicator of fraud under AU-C 240, communicate a presumed material weakness to governance, and consider the effect on the audit opinion"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Under AU-C 240.32, management override of controls is a fraud risk factor requiring heightened scrutiny regardless of dollar amount. AU-C 265.A7 indicates that management override of internal controls is at least a significant deficiency and is a strong indicator of a material weakness. The auditor must evaluate whether the override suggests intentional misstatement and consider its effect on the audit report. While choice B suggests merely proposing an adjustment, this approach ignores the fraud implications of an undocumented override.', 'hard', 4),

-- Q4: easy / L1 — Primary responsibility for internal control
(1, 'Misstatements and Control Deficiencies', 'Under auditing standards, primary responsibility for the design, implementation, and maintenance of internal control rests with:', '["The external auditor engaged to audit the financial statements", "Management and those charged with governance of the entity", "The internal audit department of the entity", "The regulatory body that oversees the entity''s industry"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Under AU-C 315.04, management is responsible for the design, implementation, and maintenance of internal control relevant to the preparation of financial statements. The external auditor evaluates controls but does not design or implement them. While choice A might seem plausible given the auditor''s role in evaluating controls, AU-C 200 clarifies that the auditor''s responsibility is to obtain reasonable assurance, not to maintain the entity''s controls.', 'easy', 1),

-- Q5: easy / L1 — Deficiency in operation definition
(1, 'Misstatements and Control Deficiencies', 'Under AU-C 265, a deficiency in the operation of an internal control exists when:', '["A required control has never been implemented by the entity", "A properly designed control does not operate effectively or is not performed by qualified personnel", "Management has identified the deficiency and implemented a corrective action plan", "The control objective is met through an alternative compensating procedure"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Under AU-C 265.06, a deficiency in operation exists when a properly designed control does not operate effectively, or when the person performing the control does not possess the necessary authority or competence to perform the control effectively. Unlike an operating deficiency, choice A describes a design deficiency because the control is missing entirely rather than malfunctioning.', 'easy', 1),

-- Q6: medium / L3 — IT control deficiency impacting multiple transaction streams
(1, 'Misstatements and Control Deficiencies', 'While auditing Cascade Digital Media, the IT auditor discovers that the general IT control over program change management failed for a three-month period, during which unauthorized modifications were made to the ERP system. The ERP processes revenue, purchasing, and payroll transactions. Analyze the most likely effect of this IT control deficiency on the audit.', '["The deficiency affects only the transaction cycle where errors were actually detected during substantive testing", "The deficiency is limited to the program change management process and does not affect financial statement assertions", "The deficiency potentially undermines the reliability of all application controls dependent on the ERP, affecting revenue, purchasing, and payroll assertions", "The deficiency requires the auditor to disclaim an opinion because IT general controls cannot be tested retroactively"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Under AU-C 315.A135, IT general controls support the effective functioning of application controls. When a pervasive IT general control such as program change management fails, the auditor cannot rely on any application controls processed by the affected system. This affects all transaction cycles running through the ERP—revenue, purchasing, and payroll. However, choice A is incorrect because limiting the impact to cycles with detected errors ignores that undetected errors may exist in other cycles.', 'medium', 3),

-- Q7: medium / L3 — Effect of corrected misstatements on risk assessment
(1, 'Misstatements and Control Deficiencies', 'During the audit of Bellwood Pharmaceuticals, the auditor discovers 12 misstatements in revenue recognition, all of which management agrees to correct. The misstatements total $85,000 against a materiality of $300,000. Despite the corrections, the engagement partner asks the team to assess whether additional procedures are needed. The nature and pattern of these corrected misstatements most likely indicates:', '["A potential deficiency in revenue recognition controls that could warrant additional testing and evaluation under AU-C 265", "No further action because all misstatements were corrected and the financial statements are now fairly presented", "A scope limitation requiring the auditor to issue a qualified opinion on revenue recognition", "That the auditor must withdraw from the engagement due to unreliable management representations"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Under AU-C 450.08, the auditor should consider whether corrected misstatements may indicate risks of additional misstatements that could be material. A pattern of 12 revenue recognition errors suggests a potential control deficiency under AU-C 265, warranting further evaluation. Although the misstatements were corrected, this does not eliminate the auditor''s responsibility to assess the underlying cause. Choice B is incorrect because correction alone does not resolve the auditor''s obligations regarding control evaluation.', 'medium', 3),

-- Q8: easy / L1 — Clearly trivial definition
(1, 'Misstatements and Control Deficiencies', 'Under AU-C 450, the term ''clearly trivial'' describes misstatements that are:', '["Material to the financial statements taken as a whole", "Below performance materiality but above overall materiality", "Immaterial individually but material when aggregated with other misstatements", "Of an amount so small that they would not need to be accumulated for further evaluation"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Under AU-C 450.A2, clearly trivial is not another expression for ''not material.'' It describes misstatements that are of a wholly different, smaller magnitude—amounts that the auditor designates as below the threshold requiring accumulation. While choice C describes misstatements that are individually immaterial but must still be accumulated, clearly trivial misstatements fall below even that level of concern.', 'easy', 1),

-- Q9: medium / L3 — Qualitative factors in misstatement evaluation
(1, 'Misstatements and Control Deficiencies', 'During the audit of Pinnacle Aerospace Corporation, the auditor identifies a $40,000 understatement of warranty expense. Overall materiality is $500,000. However, the misstatement causes Pinnacle to meet—rather than miss—an earnings-per-share target that triggers executive bonuses. Analyze the most significant factor the auditor should consider when evaluating this misstatement.', '["The misstatement is quantitatively immaterial at 8% of materiality and requires no further evaluation beyond accumulation", "The qualitative significance of the misstatement, including its effect on management compensation, may make it material despite its small dollar amount", "The misstatement must be considered material solely because it relates to an accounting estimate", "The misstatement should be excluded from the summary of uncorrected misstatements because it involves management judgment"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Under AU-C 450.A18, the auditor considers qualitative factors when evaluating uncorrected misstatements. A misstatement that enables management to meet a compensation target may be material regardless of its quantitative size because it indicates potential management bias. SAB No. 99 reinforces that qualitative factors can render quantitatively small misstatements material. However, choice A is incorrect because it limits evaluation to quantitative measures alone, ignoring the qualitative significance.', 'medium', 3),

-- Q10: medium / L2 — Summary of uncorrected misstatements communication
(1, 'Misstatements and Control Deficiencies', 'Near the conclusion of the audit of Sterling Manufacturing, the engagement team has accumulated five uncorrected misstatements totaling $65,000 against overall materiality of $200,000. Under AU-C 450, the auditor should prepare and communicate to those charged with governance:', '["A summary of all uncorrected misstatements accumulated during the audit, requesting that the misstatements be corrected", "Only those uncorrected misstatements that individually exceed performance materiality", "A summary limited to factual misstatements, excluding any judgmental or projected misstatements", "A summary that includes both corrected and uncorrected misstatements with equal emphasis"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Under AU-C 450.12, the auditor must communicate to those charged with governance all uncorrected misstatements accumulated during the audit and request that they be corrected. This includes factual, judgmental, and projected misstatements rather than only those above performance materiality. While choice B suggests filtering by performance materiality, the standard requires all accumulated uncorrected misstatements to be communicated.', 'medium', 2),

-- Q11: medium / L2 — Repeat significant deficiency from prior year
(1, 'Misstatements and Control Deficiencies', 'During the 2025 audit of Lakeview Regional Hospital, the auditor identifies a significant deficiency in the revenue cycle that was also communicated to governance during the 2024 audit. Management acknowledged the deficiency but has not yet implemented corrective action. Under AU-C 265, the auditor should:', '["Omit the deficiency from the current-year communication because governance was already informed in the prior year", "Downgrade the deficiency to a non-reportable condition because management acknowledged it", "Communicate the significant deficiency again in writing, noting that it was previously communicated and has not been remediated", "Automatically elevate the deficiency to a material weakness because it has persisted for two consecutive years"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Under AU-C 265.A15, if a previously communicated significant deficiency has not been remediated, the auditor should communicate it again in the current year. Although choice D may seem logical, repetition alone does not automatically elevate a significant deficiency to a material weakness—the auditor must evaluate severity based on the likelihood and magnitude of potential misstatement.', 'medium', 2),

-- Q12: medium / L3 — Evaluating management bias in misstatement correction patterns
(1, 'Misstatements and Control Deficiencies', 'While reviewing the aggregate of uncorrected misstatements for the 2025 audit of Northfield Consumer Products, the senior auditor notices that all seven uncorrected misstatements increase reported net income, while the four misstatements management agreed to correct all decreased net income. Analyze what this pattern most likely suggests about the auditor''s evaluation of misstatements.', '["The pattern is coincidental and has no bearing on the auditor''s evaluation since total uncorrected misstatements are below materiality", "The auditor should focus only on the quantitative total of uncorrected misstatements without considering directional patterns", "The auditor must automatically issue an adverse opinion when directional bias exists in uncorrected misstatements", "The pattern may indicate management bias under AU-C 450, requiring the auditor to reconsider whether uncorrected misstatements are material in the aggregate"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Under AU-C 450.A19, the auditor evaluates whether uncorrected misstatements indicate a bias by management. When all uncorrected misstatements favor higher income while all corrections reduce income, this directional pattern suggests management may be selectively correcting misstatements to inflate earnings. However, choice A is incorrect because dismissing the pattern as coincidental ignores the auditor''s professional skepticism obligations under AU-C 200.', 'medium', 3),

-- Q13: medium / L3 — Control environment deficiency (tone at the top)
(1, 'Misstatements and Control Deficiencies', 'During the audit of Redstone Energy Partners, the engagement team observes that the CEO frequently dismisses internal audit findings and has publicly criticized the audit committee for raising control concerns. The company''s code of conduct exists on paper but is not reinforced through training or enforcement actions. Analyze the most appropriate classification of this observation under auditing standards.', '["An inherent limitation of internal control that does not constitute a reportable deficiency", "An entity-level control environment deficiency that is at least a significant deficiency and may indicate a material weakness", "A management style preference that falls outside the scope of the auditor''s evaluation of internal control", "A deficiency in monitoring activities that should be reported to management but not to governance"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Under AU-C 265.A12, deficiencies in the control environment—such as management''s tone at the top—are entity-level deficiencies that are at least significant deficiencies. When management actively undermines the control environment by dismissing audit findings and failing to enforce the code of conduct, the deficiency is pervasive and may constitute a material weakness. Unlike an inherent limitation (choice A), this deficiency results from management''s deliberate actions in weakening the control environment.', 'medium', 3),

-- Q14: hard / L4 — Pervasive vs material distinction for opinion modification
(1, 'Misstatements and Control Deficiencies', 'The auditor of Summit Ridge Development has identified $350,000 in aggregate uncorrected misstatements against overall materiality of $250,000. The misstatements are concentrated in revenue recognition and accounts receivable, representing 4% of total revenue and 8% of total receivables. The auditor has determined the misstatements are material but must evaluate whether they are also pervasive. Evaluate which conclusion is most appropriate.', '["The misstatements are material but not pervasive because they are confined to specific accounts, supporting a qualified rather than adverse opinion", "The misstatements are automatically pervasive because they exceed materiality, requiring an adverse opinion", "The misstatements cannot be evaluated for pervasiveness until management provides a written response declining to make corrections", "The misstatements are neither material nor pervasive because they represent less than 10% of both revenue and receivables"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Under AU-C 705.06, the auditor distinguishes between material misstatements (exceeding materiality) and pervasive misstatements (those not confined to specific elements, accounts, or items, or that represent a substantial proportion of the financial statements). Since the misstatements are concentrated in revenue and receivables rather than spread across multiple areas, a qualified opinion under AU-C 705.08 is appropriate. However, choice B is incorrect because exceeding materiality does not automatically make misstatements pervasive.', 'hard', 4),

-- Q15: easy / L1 — Timing of control deficiency communications
(1, 'Misstatements and Control Deficiencies', 'Under AU-C 265, written communication of significant deficiencies and material weaknesses to governance must be made:', '["Within five business days of identifying the deficiency during fieldwork", "Before the auditor begins substantive testing procedures", "No later than 60 days following the report release date", "Only upon specific request from those charged with governance"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Under AU-C 265.13, the auditor''s written communication of significant deficiencies and material weaknesses must be made no later than 60 days following the report release date. This timing allows the auditor to complete the evaluation of all identified deficiencies before communicating. While choice A suggests communicating within five business days of identification, this is not required by the standard and could lead to incomplete or premature assessments.', 'easy', 1),

-- Q16: hard / L4 — PCAOB integrated audit material weakness classification
(1, 'Misstatements and Control Deficiencies', 'During an integrated audit of Vanguard Systems Inc., a publicly traded company, the audit team identifies a material weakness in the company''s financial close process. Management disagrees with the classification, arguing the deficiency is only a significant deficiency because no actual misstatement resulted. Under PCAOB AS 2201, evaluate the auditor''s appropriate response.', '["Accept management''s reclassification because the absence of a resulting misstatement confirms the deficiency is not a material weakness", "Defer the classification decision to the audit committee since management and the auditor disagree", "Issue a clean report on internal control since management has represented that the deficiency will be remediated before year-end", "Maintain the material weakness classification based on the reasonable possibility of material misstatement, regardless of whether an actual misstatement occurred"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Under PCAOB AS 2201.A11, a material weakness exists when there is a reasonable possibility that a material misstatement would not be prevented or detected on a timely basis. The classification depends on the potential for misstatement rather than whether one actually occurred. Although choice A reflects management''s argument, it misapplies the standard by requiring an actual misstatement when the test is based on likelihood and magnitude of potential misstatement.', 'hard', 4),

-- Q17: easy / L1 — Management representation letter regarding uncorrected misstatements
(1, 'Misstatements and Control Deficiencies', 'Under AU-C 580, the written representation letter from management regarding uncorrected misstatements must include:', '["Management''s belief that the effects of uncorrected misstatements are immaterial, individually and in the aggregate, to the financial statements", "A statement that all misstatements discovered during the audit have been corrected in the financial statements", "An acknowledgment that the auditor has sole responsibility for determining which misstatements require correction", "A certification that management has identified all misstatements in the financial statements"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Under AU-C 580.A11, the written representation letter should include management''s acknowledgment that the effects of uncorrected misstatements are immaterial, individually and in the aggregate, to the financial statements as a whole. A summary of such items is typically attached to the representation letter. Choice B is incorrect because the representation specifically addresses uncorrected misstatements that management has chosen not to correct, rather than asserting all misstatements were corrected.', 'easy', 1),

-- Q18: medium / L2 — Projected misstatement calculation using ratio estimation
(1, 'Misstatements and Control Deficiencies', 'During the audit of Bayshore Electronics, the auditor tests a sample of 40 accounts receivable confirmations from a population with a recorded balance of $2,400,000. The sample has a recorded balance of $320,000, and the auditor identifies misstatements totaling $16,000 in the sample. Using the ratio method of projection, the projected misstatement for the population is:', '["$16,000", "$120,000", "$960,000", "$60,000"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Under AU-C 530.A55, the ratio method projects sample misstatements to the population by multiplying the misstatement ratio by the population value. The misstatement rate is $16,000 / $320,000 = 5%. Projected misstatement = 5% x $2,400,000 = $120,000. While choice A ($16,000) represents the actual sample misstatement, it incorrectly treats the sample result as the total projected misstatement without extrapolation to the full population.', 'medium', 2),

-- Q19: medium / L3 — Sampling vs nonsampling misstatement classification
(1, 'Misstatements and Control Deficiencies', 'The audit team at Crestline Insurance discovers two misstatements during testing. The first, a $22,000 error, was found in a sample of policy premium calculations and must be projected to the population. The second, a $45,000 error, was found during a 100% examination of claims exceeding $500,000. Analyze how each misstatement should be classified in the auditor''s summary of misstatements.', '["Both misstatements are projected misstatements because they were identified through audit procedures", "Both misstatements are factual misstatements because the auditor has identified specific dollar amounts", "The $22,000 error generates a projected misstatement based on extrapolation, while the $45,000 error is a factual misstatement requiring no projection", "The $22,000 error is a judgmental misstatement, while the $45,000 error is a projected misstatement"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Under AU-C 450.06, factual misstatements are those about which there is no doubt, typically identified in items tested 100%. Projected misstatements arise from extrapolating sample results to the population. The $45,000 error found in a complete examination is factual, while the $22,000 sample error requires projection under AU-C 530. Although choice B identifies specific dollar amounts for both, the sample error must be extrapolated rather than treated as factual because it represents only a portion of the population.', 'medium', 3),

-- Q20: easy / L2 — Communication of lesser control deficiencies to management
(1, 'Misstatements and Control Deficiencies', 'During the audit of Maplewood Community Bank, the auditor identifies several control deficiencies that do not rise to the level of significant deficiencies or material weaknesses. Under AU-C 265, the auditor''s obligation regarding these lesser deficiencies is to:', '["Include them in the same written communication as significant deficiencies and material weaknesses", "Report them to the SEC within 30 days of identification", "Disclose them in the auditor''s report on the financial statements", "Consider communicating them to management, either orally or in writing"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Under AU-C 265.11, the auditor should consider whether to communicate control deficiencies that are not significant deficiencies or material weaknesses to management. This communication may be oral or written and is at the auditor''s discretion. Unlike significant deficiencies and material weaknesses, these lesser deficiencies are not required to be included in the formal written communication to governance (choice A).', 'easy', 2),

-- Q21: medium / L2 — Effect of misstatement on debt covenant compliance
(1, 'Misstatements and Control Deficiencies', 'Timberline Construction has a loan agreement requiring a current ratio of at least 1.5:1. Before audit adjustments, the company reports current assets of $3,600,000 and current liabilities of $2,300,000, yielding a current ratio of 1.57:1. The auditor identifies an uncorrected misstatement that would reclassify $200,000 from current assets to noncurrent assets. Under AU-C 450, the auditor should evaluate this misstatement by considering:', '["Whether the misstatement, though below overall materiality, causes noncompliance with the debt covenant", "Only the quantitative effect relative to overall materiality, without regard to contractual obligations", "Whether the loan agreement is a related party transaction requiring separate disclosure", "Only whether the misstatement affects earnings per share calculations"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Under AU-C 450.A18, the auditor considers qualitative factors when evaluating uncorrected misstatements, including their effect on compliance with contractual requirements such as debt covenants. The reclassification would reduce the current ratio to 1.48:1 ($3,400,000 / $2,300,000), triggering a covenant violation. While choice B focuses only on quantitative materiality, the standard requires the auditor to also consider qualitative consequences such as covenant noncompliance.', 'medium', 2),

-- Q22: easy / L1 — Known (factual) misstatement definition
(1, 'Misstatements and Control Deficiencies', 'In audit terminology, a known misstatement refers to:', '["A misstatement that management has acknowledged and agreed to correct", "A misstatement identified through audit procedures about which there is no doubt regarding its existence and amount", "A misstatement that the auditor suspects exists but cannot quantify precisely", "A misstatement disclosed in the prior-year management representation letter"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Under AU-C 450.A4, a known (factual) misstatement is one about which there is no doubt—the auditor has identified a specific, quantifiable error through testing. This contrasts with projected misstatements (extrapolated from samples) and judgmental misstatements (involving differences over estimates). While choice A describes management''s response to a misstatement, it does not define the misstatement''s classification as known or factual.', 'easy', 1),

-- Q23: medium / L2 — Summary of audit differences purpose
(1, 'Misstatements and Control Deficiencies', 'During the wrap-up phase of the audit of Fieldstone Properties, the engagement manager prepares a schedule listing all identified misstatements, noting which were corrected by management and which remain uncorrected. Under AU-C 450, the primary purpose of maintaining this summary of audit differences is to:', '["Fulfill the auditor''s obligation to publish all discovered misstatements in the audit report", "Provide a basis for calculating the auditor''s fee based on the number of adjustments processed", "Enable the auditor to evaluate whether uncorrected misstatements are material individually or in the aggregate", "Document management''s agreement that all financial statements are free from error"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Under AU-C 450.11, the auditor must evaluate whether uncorrected misstatements are material, individually or in the aggregate. The summary of audit differences serves as the basis for this evaluation by accumulating all identified misstatements in one schedule. Choice A is incorrect because the auditor does not publish individual misstatements in the audit report, unlike the summary which is an internal working paper.', 'medium', 2),

-- Q24: easy / L1 — Three categories of misstatements under AU-C 450
(1, 'Misstatements and Control Deficiencies', 'AU-C 450 identifies three categories of misstatements that an auditor may encounter during an audit. These categories are:', '["Intentional, unintentional, and systemic misstatements", "Quantitative, qualitative, and pervasive misstatements", "Material, immaterial, and clearly trivial misstatements", "Factual, judgmental, and projected misstatements"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Under AU-C 450.06, misstatements are classified as factual (no doubt about existence), judgmental (differences arising from management''s judgments about estimates or accounting policies the auditor considers unreasonable), and projected (the auditor''s best estimate of misstatements in populations based on projecting sample results). Although choice C lists valid materiality thresholds, these describe the significance of misstatements rather than their classification categories.', 'easy', 1),

-- Q25: medium / L3 — Scope limitation effect on misstatement evaluation
(1, 'Misstatements and Control Deficiencies', 'While auditing Riverbend Logistics, the auditor is unable to observe the physical inventory count due to a client-imposed restriction. Inventory represents 35% of total assets, and the auditor cannot apply sufficient alternative procedures. Analyze how this scope limitation affects the auditor''s evaluation of potential misstatements in the financial statements.', '["The auditor cannot determine the magnitude of potential misstatements in inventory, which may require a qualified or disclaimer of opinion under AU-C 705", "The scope limitation is irrelevant to misstatement evaluation because no specific misstatement has been identified", "The auditor should project estimated misstatements for inventory based on error rates found in other asset categories", "The limitation only affects the auditor''s control testing and has no impact on the opinion on the financial statements"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Under AU-C 705.09, when the auditor is unable to obtain sufficient appropriate audit evidence due to a scope limitation, the possible effects on the financial statements could be material and pervasive. Since inventory is 35% of total assets, the inability to verify it creates uncertainty about potential misstatements that directly affects the auditor''s opinion. However, choice C is not acceptable because projecting errors from other accounts ignores that inventory has unique characteristics and risks.', 'medium', 3),

-- Q26: medium / L2 — Entity-level vs process-level control deficiencies
(1, 'Misstatements and Control Deficiencies', 'During the audit of Pacific Rim Trading Company, the auditor identifies two deficiencies: (1) the company lacks a formal risk assessment process, and (2) a clerk in accounts payable does not verify three-way matches before processing invoices above $5,000. Under AU-C 265, classify these deficiencies.', '["Both deficiencies are process-level deficiencies because they relate to specific transaction processing weaknesses", "The lack of a risk assessment process is an entity-level deficiency, while the three-way match failure is a process-level deficiency", "Both deficiencies are entity-level deficiencies because they indicate a weak overall control environment", "The lack of a risk assessment process is a process-level deficiency, while the three-way match failure is an entity-level deficiency"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Under AU-C 265.A5, entity-level controls operate across the organization and include the control environment, risk assessment process, and monitoring activities. The absence of a formal risk assessment process is an entity-level deficiency. Process-level controls operate at the transaction level, such as the three-way match in accounts payable. Although choice A classifies both as process-level, the risk assessment gap affects the entire organization rather than a single transaction stream.', 'medium', 2),

-- Q27: hard / L4 — GAGAS noncompliance-related control deficiency reporting
(1, 'Misstatements and Control Deficiencies', 'During a Government Auditing Standards (Yellow Book) engagement for the City of Havenwood, the auditor identifies a control deficiency that resulted in $18,000 of federal grant expenditures being incurred outside the allowable cost categories. The grant totals $2,000,000 and the questioned costs are below the $25,000 reporting threshold in the compliance supplement. Evaluate the auditor''s reporting obligation.', '["No reporting is required because the questioned costs are below the compliance supplement threshold", "Report the finding only in the management letter since it does not meet the Yellow Book reporting threshold", "Report the finding in the audit report because GAGAS requires communication of all known questioned costs and the related control deficiency regardless of a fixed-dollar threshold", "Defer reporting until the next audit to determine whether the deficiency is a recurring issue"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Under GAGAS 6.40, auditors must report known questioned costs when the auditor identifies noncompliance with provisions of laws, regulations, contracts, or grant agreements. The Yellow Book requires reporting significant deficiencies, material weaknesses, and instances of noncompliance that are material to the financial statements or program. While choice A applies compliance supplement thresholds, GAGAS independently requires reporting known questioned costs regardless of those thresholds.', 'hard', 4),

-- Q28: hard / L4 — Compensating controls for IT deficiency assessment
(1, 'Misstatements and Control Deficiencies', 'The auditor of Atlas Manufacturing discovers that the company''s IT department lacks formal access controls over the general ledger system, allowing all accounting staff to post journal entries without approval. However, the controller independently reviews and approves a daily printout of all journal entries exceeding $1,000 before the monthly close. Management asserts the compensating control mitigates the access control weakness. Evaluate the auditor''s assessment.', '["The compensating control fully mitigates the deficiency because the controller reviews all significant entries, eliminating the need to report the access control weakness", "The compensating control is irrelevant because IT access control deficiencies must always be classified as material weaknesses", "The compensating control eliminates the deficiency entirely because detective controls are considered superior to preventive controls", "The compensating control reduces but does not eliminate the deficiency because entries below $1,000 are not reviewed and unauthorized access remains a design weakness"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Under AU-C 265.A8, compensating controls may reduce the severity of a deficiency but should be evaluated for whether they fully address the risk. The $1,000 threshold leaves smaller entries unreviewed, and numerous small unauthorized entries could aggregate to a material amount. The underlying lack of access controls remains a design deficiency. While choice A suggests the compensating control fully mitigates the weakness, it overstates the mitigating effect by ignoring the gap in coverage for entries below the review threshold.', 'hard', 4),

-- Q29: easy / L1 — Tolerable misstatement definition
(1, 'Misstatements and Control Deficiencies', 'In audit sampling, tolerable misstatement is defined as:', '["The maximum misstatement in a population that the auditor can accept and still conclude the sampling objective has been achieved", "The total amount of all misstatements identified during the audit engagement", "The threshold below which misstatements are considered clearly trivial", "The amount of misstatement that management has agreed not to correct"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Under AU-C 530.05, tolerable misstatement is the application of performance materiality to a particular sampling procedure—it represents the maximum monetary error in an account balance or class of transactions that the auditor will accept. Although choice C also describes a threshold concept, the clearly trivial threshold under AU-C 450 is a separate and much lower amount used for accumulation purposes rather than sampling precision.', 'easy', 1),

-- Q30: medium / L2 — Misstatement direction evaluation
(1, 'Misstatements and Control Deficiencies', 'During the 2025 audit of Grandview Hospitality Group, the auditor accumulates five uncorrected misstatements: three overstate assets by $15,000, $22,000, and $18,000, while two understate liabilities by $12,000 and $9,000. Overall materiality is $200,000. Under AU-C 450, the auditor should evaluate the net effect of these misstatements on income as:', '["$36,000 because only the asset overstatements affect income", "$76,000 because all five misstatements overstate net income when combined", "$55,000 because only the three largest misstatements should be aggregated", "$21,000 because the liability understatements partially offset the asset overstatements"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Under AU-C 450.11, the auditor evaluates the aggregate effect of uncorrected misstatements on the financial statements. Asset overstatements ($15,000 + $22,000 + $18,000 = $55,000) and liability understatements ($12,000 + $9,000 = $21,000) both overstate net income, so they compound rather than offset. Although choice D treats these as offsetting, both categories of error inflate income in the same direction, yielding a combined overstatement of $76,000.', 'medium', 2)
;

COMMIT;
